// §Fase 122.d — the program that makes `cache` real.
//
// A `pure` tool, a module-default cache, and a flow that calls the tool TWICE
// with identical arguments. `fase122_d_cache_hits.rs` deploys this through the
// real `/v1/deploy` + `/v1/execute` handlers and counts hits on a live upstream.
// The whole sub-fase is the difference between two and one.
//
// Before §122.d the answer was two. §85 shipped the grammar, the four safety
// laws (`axon-T863`–`T867`), the audit vocabulary and a production-hardened
// cache core with its own passing tests — and deferred the last step (§85.h,
// "deferred, not built"). §122.a measured the result: `CacheRuntime::dispatch`
// had ZERO production callers, so `ttl:`, `key_params:`, `invalidate_on:` and
// `default_policy:` were inert and `backend: redis` named a tier that did not
// exist. It shipped as `Real` in 2.88.0.
//
// `runtime: enrich` is RELATIVE on purpose. §Fase 58.g resolves a relative
// runtime against the deployment's tool base URL, which is how the test points
// it at a local counting server — and `axon-T949` forbids an absolute URL in
// source anyway: a production URL with no lifetime, no capacity and no shield
// is an ungoverned channel.

tool Enrich {
    provider: http
    runtime: enrich
    effects: <pure>
    output_type: String
    parameters: { id: String }
}

// No `ttl:`. Sound only because the tool is provably `pure` — `axon-T865`
// refuses a ttl-less cache over anything that is not (D85.9: provable-forever,
// never non-deterministic-forever).
cache Memo { default: true }

flow Run() -> Unit {
    use Enrich(id = "ada")
    use Enrich(id = "ada")
}
